41	APPOINTMENT OF PRESIDENTIAL SECRETARIAT LEADERSHIP TEAM	chief-justice-emeritus-david-maraga-announces-senior-appointments	Latest News	\N	/uploads/3bbf2295-bbcd-4114-9d17-290ee171a3cd.webp	<figure class="image"><img src="/uploads/3c09e6ca-2187-40ea-a8e8-481c7948ab17.jpg" width="2550" height="3300"/></figure>	published	2026-07-19 10:40:00	1	t	2026-07-20 16:40:39	2026-08-17 22:38:06	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
25	Answering the Call to Lead-David Maraga 	answering-the-call-to-lead-david-maraga-for-president	Blog	David Maraga President	/uploads/cadf01b0-4d0c-4613-a774-399a315ae5dd.jpg	<p style="text-align: justify"><i><strong>A Career of  Leadership and  Intergity</strong></i></p><p style="text-align: justify">David Kenani Maraga has spent more than four decades in public service and the law, building a reputation as one of Kenya&#39;s most principled and respected leaders. Born in Nyamira County, he studied law at the University of Nairobi before practising as an advocate for over 25 years. His career on the bench culminated in his appointment as Kenya&#39;s 14th Chief Justice and President of the Supreme Court, where he became known for his unwavering commitment to the Constitution, judicial independence, and the rule of law. </p><p style="text-align: justify">Throughout his career, h<strong>e has maintained a reputation for integrity, humility, and service, guided by a deep conviction that no individual or institution stands above the law. </strong></p><p style="text-align: justify">As Chief Justice, Maraga presided over one of the most consequential periods in Kenya&#39;s constitutional history. He led the Supreme Court in the landmark 2017 decision that annulled a presidential election, the first ruling of its kind in Africa, <strong>affirming that democratic legitimacy depends on constitutional compliance rather than political power</strong>. </p><p style="text-align: justify">He also advised the dissolution of Parliament for failing to implement the Constitution&#39;s two thirds gender principle, championed reforms that modernized the Judiciary through digitization and faster case management, and consistently defended the independence of the courts in the face of political pressure.</p><p style="text-align: justify">Across more than 1,250 judgments and numerous landmark decisions, his judicial philosophy reflected constitutional courage, procedural fairness, and a steadfast commitment to <strong>protecting the rights and dignity of every Kenyan. </strong></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><i><strong>Continued Activism</strong></i></p><p style="text-align: justify">Since leaving the Judiciary, Maraga has continued to stand with the people of Kenya. He has spoken out against corruption, police brutality, environmental degragradation, human rights violations,  impunity, and the abuse of public office. He has <strong>advocated for stronger public institutions, equal opportunity, environmental protection, devolution, and the rights and dignity of every Kenyan. </strong></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><strong>Answering the Call to Lead</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify">The 2024 Kenya Gen Z protests against the Finance Bill, the rising cost of living, and the human rights violations that followed, including killings, abductions, and enforced disappearances, became a defining moment of reflection for Maraga. He was devastated by what he witnessed.</p><p style="text-align: justify">Young Kenyans, especially Gen Z, called on him to offer the leadership the country was yearning for. After deep introspection, he answered that call with <strong>one clear mission: to restore constitutional order, uphold the rule of law, and lead Kenya back to justice, integrity, and hope.</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify">Maraga formally announced his candidacy in early 2025 and has since embarked on a nationwide journey to listen to Kenyans, understand their hopes and challenges, and build a people driven movement of <i><strong>Ukombozi (</strong></i><strong>deliverance).</strong> </p><p style="text-align: justify">Guided by the vision to <strong>Reset, Restore and Rebuild Kenya</strong>, the movement is committed to renewing the nation&#39;s promise through accountable leadership, justice, and shared prosperity. </p><p style="text-align: justify">Through his travels across the country, listening to citizens in towns, villages, markets, universities, places of worship, and community forums, he has encountered <strong>a nation weighed down by rising unemployment, high cost of living, corruption, failing public services, insecurity, and a growing loss of faith in leadership. </strong></p><p style="text-align: justify"><strong>It is these experiences that reminded him that Kenya&#39;s challenges are not caused by a lack of talent or resources, but by a failure of leadership fueled by lack of integrity and accountability. </strong></p><p style="text-align: justify">This conviction led David Maraga to seek the Presidency of the Republic of Kenya. He believes that the country stands at a defining moment and that<strong> its future depends on restoring trust in public leadership and returning government to its constitutional purpose of serving the people</strong>. He offers a style of leadership rooted in honesty, justice, competence, and accountability. </p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><i><strong>A Vision for Kenya</strong></i></p><p style="text-align: justify">Maraga&#39;s presidency is guided by his philosophy of <i>Utkatiba Ndio Tiba</i> -(constitutionalism) as the anchor of good governance.</p><p style="text-align: justify"><strong>He believes that public office is a sacred trust, that every public shilling must be protected, that every Kenyan deserves equal treatment before the law, and that government should create an environment where enterprise, innovation, education, and hard work can flourish</strong>.</p><p style="text-align: justify">His vision is that of a Kenya where institutions are stronger than individuals, where young people can build successful lives at home, where every region shares fairly in national development, and where future generations inherit a country that is more united, prosperous, and just, than the one we know today.</p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><strong>The United Green Movement Party (UGM)</strong></p><p style="text-align: justify">David Maraga is contesting the presidency on the ticket of the <strong>United Green Movement (UGM) Party, </strong>a party whose vision of constitutionalism, social justice, environmental stewardship, democratic governance, and accountable leadership closely reflects the principles that have guided his entire career. </p><p style="text-align: justify">The United Green Movement, is one of <strong>the few political parties in Kenya whose ideology genuinely aligns with his lifelong commitment to the rule of law, human rights, integrity in public service, and respect for the Constitution.</strong> The party&#39;s vision of building a united, peaceful, and prosperous Kenya where every citizen lives with dignity and opportunity reflects a future he believes is possible for our country.</p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><i><strong>A call to all Kenyans </strong></i></p><p style="text-align: justify">This campaign is <strong>an invitation to every Kenyan who believes that our nation can be governed with honesty, competence, and integrity.</strong> </p><p style="text-align: justify">It is a call to young people seeking opportunity, to workers and entrepreneurs striving for a fair economy, to families longing for security and justice, and to every citizen who believes that the Constitution must be more than words on paper. </p><p style="text-align: justify"><strong>David Maraga invites all Kenyans to support this campaign</strong>, and help build a Kenya where public service is defined by accountability, leadership is measured by character, and every generation can look to the future with confidence and hope.</p>	published	2026-04-01 16:04:00	1	f	2026-07-01 13:05:19	2026-08-03 20:25:47	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
42	STATEMENT BY HON DAVID MARAGA IN RESPONSE TO PRESIDENT RUTO'S ADDRESS TO THE NATION	statement-by-hon-david-maraga-in-response-to-president-ruto-s-address-to-the-nation	Press Releases	\N	\N	<p style="text-align: justify"><span>Fellow Kenyans,</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Before I respond to what President Ruto told you tonight, I want to ask you a simple question, and I want you to answer it honestly in your own home, in your own heart. Are you better off today than you were when William Ruto took office? Look at your table. Look at your payslip, if you still have one. Look at your children&#39;s school fees. Look at the price of unga, of fuel, of everything that keeps a family alive. Are you better off? I already know your answer. </span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>It is no.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>That question is the one question President Ruto has completely failed to answer tonight and instead tried to sell you more False Hope.  I will not let him.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>First, let me be direct about where we stand.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Time is up for William Ruto. No policy announcement, no political arrangement, no reshuffling of alliances can dissuade Kenyans from the one mission that now unites us across every county and every community: removing him from power. This is not politics as usual. This is a response to real pain. Kenyans have been killed. Kenyans have been abducted. Corruption has  worsened with this President at its very center, not at its margins. The cost of living has crushed households under the weight of reckless governance and reckless borrowing. This is not a policy disagreement. It is a national emergency, and Kenyans know it.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Second, let me tell you what comes after him.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>From 2027 to 2032, the leadership that replaces this administration must have one overriding priority above all others: resetting this country to the rule of law and to constitutionalism. I say this plainly because no policy, no vision, however well articulaed, and no programme, however lofty its promises, will succeed in a country run by cartels whose only interest is enriching themselves and harming anyone who stands in their way. </span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>You cannot build an economy on a foundation that criminal networks control. You cannot promise prosperity while cartels sit at the center of government. The first task of the next government is not a stadium or a highway. It is restoring a Kenya where the law applies to everyone, including the President.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Third, accountability will not be optional.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Those who have looted this country&#39;s resources with impunity must be held to account through strong penal sanctions, and every shilling of stolen and corruptly acquired wealth returned to the Kenyan people. Not promises. Not new visions. Not task forces that go nowhere. Real consequences, and real restitution. Kenyans have watched thieves walk free for too long while ordinary citizens are jailed for stealing bread. That era ends.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Now, to what the President said tonight.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>He recited a list of projects and lofty, aspirations as proof of progress. I say to you: a stadium without investment in our athletes, our footballers, our young talent, is a monument built for headlines, not for our children&#39;s futures. A market built without protecting the farmer from exploitation is an empty shell dressed up for a photograph. Brick and mortar are not development when the people living beside them cannot afford a meal or use them.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Government statistics claiming the economy is growing while te majority are starving are empty rhetoric. Kenya will grow, but it will grow at its own pace, led by its own people, not through Ruto&#39;s punitive taxation and endless political posturing about how to become a first world country.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>And the subjects he avoided entirely tonight: the runaway corruption, the rogue police units, the hit squads, the abductions used to silence dissent, and the human rights violations that have made Kenyans afraid to speak in their own country are the pillars on which we will rise. Ruto will not mention them because he cannot defend them. He doesn’t believe in them. Or this Constitution.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Kenyans should ask themselves again tonight, after this address if they are better off. And if the answer is no, know that you are not alone, and know that a different Kenya, governed by law, accountable to its people, and free of state capture, is not just possible. It is coming.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"><strong>By David K. Maraga, </strong></span><span><strong>Chief Justice Emeritus &amp; UGM Co-Party Leader</strong></span></p>	published	2026-07-31 21:25:00	1	f	2026-08-04 07:26:18	2026-08-04 07:27:22	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
43	Alarming State Unpreparedness for Impending 2026/2027 “Godzila” El Niño	alarming-state-unpreparedness-for-impending-2026-2027-godzila-el-ni-o	Press Releases	\N	\N	<p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"><i>The United Green Movement (UGM) Party and the 2027 Presidential Flag-bearer Retired Chief Justice David Kenani Maraga, warn that the Government is sleepwalking the country into a predictable climate disaster by failing to plan ahead of the forecasted “Godzilla” El Niño rains. </i></span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: #1A1A1A">The Kenya Meteorological Department, the World Meteorological Organization, and the regional climate authority, ICPAC, have all warned that Kenya will experience the most extreme El Niño rain season in 150 years. In their forecast, the “Godzilla El Niño” has an 8-in-10 chance of sweeping the region from October this year through to early next year. </span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p><span style="color: #1A1A1A">The United Nations has already listed Kenya among 22 countries facing the highest humanitarian risk from the calamity, and launched a $202 million global appeal to assist 8.8 million vulnerable Kenyans</span><span style="color: black">. </span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>This year’s forecast predicts worse rains than the</span><span style="color: black"> historic 1997 El </span><span>Niño. Yet, despite </span><span style="color: black">the alarm sounded by the experts there is no visible contingency preparation by the government to mitigate the looming disaster.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">Specifically, the country is staring at the following <strong>national risks</strong> which have been flagged by experts:</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">• <strong>Agriculture and Food Security: </strong>Smallholder farmers make up over 70% of the sector. Unharvested maize across Western Kenya and the Rift Valley faces rot, sprouting, and aflatoxin contamination, threatening national grain reserves and opening the door to corrupt import schemes.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">• <strong>Infrastructure and Ecology: </strong>Riverine flooding is expected along the Tana, Athi, Ewaso Nyiro, Rift Valley, and Lake Victoria basins, with severe water backflow threatening Homa Bay, Kisumu, and Nakuru. West Pokot, Elgeyo Marakwet, Nakuru, and Murang&#39;a face landslide risk, and bridges, schools, and markets face washouts.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">• <strong>Public Health: </strong>Damaged water and sanitation systems raise the risk of cholera, typhoid, and malaria outbreaks in informal settlements and marginalized rural areas. Failure to pre-position medical supplies now would be indefensible.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">Failure to prepare is not ignorance, it is negligence. The State cannot wait for floods, landslides, and disease outbreaks, then rush in with cameras and corrupt “emergency” tenders after lives and livelihoods have already been destroyed.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">We demand immediate, visible action: a national plan to protect smallholder farmers and food reserves; urgent release of county funds to clear drainage, reinforcement of riverbanks, </span><span>standby </span><span style="color: black">local emergency response mechanism; pre-positioning of essential health supplies in high-risk areas; and a clear evacuation and relocation roadmap for communities living in known flood and landslide zones</span><span>.</span><span style="color: black"> </span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">At the heart of this effort must be the urgent passage and implementation of the Ukombozi Petition presented to Parliament, which provides a framework to mobilise millions of unemployed young people for the mass manual labour required—digging drainages, strengthening riverbanks, building water-harvesting pans, and restoring local infrastructure—to get the country ready before the El Niño strikes.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">Consequently, the UGM Party demands the implementation of the following urgent measures:</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"><strong>I. Urgent Post-Harvest Agricultural Intervention: </strong>Deploy government-backed mobile drying units and mechanical harvesters to the North Rift and Western regions by August 10, 2026, procured transparently and allocated by need, not political loyalty.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"> <strong>II. Immediate Release of County Mitigation Funds: </strong>Clear outstanding Treasury allocations so counties can activate Anticipatory Disaster Risk Mechanisms and establish 500,000 decentralized water-harvesting pans in ASAL counties within three months.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"><strong>III. Healthcare Stabilization and Medical Stockpiling: </strong>KEMSA and the Ministry of Health must pre-position water purification kits, anti-malarial medication, and cholera vaccines with a six-month buffer stock, with zero tolerance for procurement scandals.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"> <strong>IV. Civil Protection and Human Rights-Centered Relocation: </strong>Under Article 43 of the Constitution, publish a non-coercive evacuation and resettlement roadmap for landslide-prone and flood-plain communities in Murang&#39;a, the Rift Valley, Tana River, Nyando and Budalang&#39;i.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"> <strong>V. Strategic Water and Resource Harvesting: </strong>Harvest excess waters from Mt. Kenya and the Aberdares through decentralized, solar-powered water-harvesting pans, turning floodwaters into productive ASAL green belts rather than another opaque mega-project.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">The UGM Party will hold the government to account for every death, every washed-away bridge, every destroyed crop and every preventable disease outbreak that is a direct consequence of state negligence and failure to prepare.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>And here we want to send a clear message to the government: we will not allow any state officials to turn a national emergency into business opportunities for politically connected cartels to divert or misappropriate funds earmarked for this disaster prevention. All officials will be held to account for their actions or omissions. </span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span>Thank you.</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">Signed,</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black"><strong>Hon. David Kenani Maraga, EGH  </strong></span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">2027 Presidential Flag-bearer &amp; Co-Party Leader</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"><span style="color: black">United Green Movement (UGM) Party</span></p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p><p style="text-align: center"> </p><p style="text-align: justify"> </p>	published	2026-07-28 10:41:00	1	f	2026-08-04 07:39:08	2026-08-04 07:39:08	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
75	MARAGA RELEASES TASKFORCE  REPORT ON POLICE REFORMS	maraga-releases-taskforce-report-on-police-reforms	Latest News	\N	/uploads/c1929f4f-d8cb-4e64-a878-b2da1ac07796.jpg	\N	published	2026-08-31 12:38:00	1	t	2026-09-03 16:41:14	2026-09-03 17:20:01	{"{\\"id\\": \\"abbff286-5686-4785-9dac-e2f8d02ce38b\\", \\"body\\": null, \\"heading\\": null, \\"position\\": 0, \\"image_urls\\": [\\"/uploads/f154c797-5252-4d74-a87c-9fc92aa237e6.pdf\\"]}","{\\"id\\": \\"3ab4c86b-31d7-428e-ad6f-426a38320dba\\", \\"body\\": \\"<p>Read Full Report Available Here <a href=\\\\\\"https://maragareport.filedrop.me/s/policereforms\\\\\\"><span style=\\\\\\"color: hsl(0,75%,60%)\\\\\\">https://maragareport.filedrop.me/s/policereforms</span></a></p>\\", \\"heading\\": null, \\"position\\": 1, \\"image_urls\\": []}"}	\N	\N	45	5	\N	\N
47	The Ukatiba Caravan: 43 Counties, One Message	the-ukatiba-caravan-43-counties-one-message	Latest News	\N	/uploads/7055819b-f3ad-4da0-9d92-64cde85cbb26.jpg	<p><span style="color: hsl(0,0%,0%)">Between February and April this year, Hon Maraga commonly known as CJ, led the UGM Party on a Ukatiba Caravan through 43 counties, not by helicopter but by road, so that no community, however remote, was skipped. From Lamu on the coast to Tangulbei in Baringo, Samburu to the shores of Lake Victoria, the Caravan combined voter registration drives with civic education on the 2010 Constitution. </span></p><p><span style="color: hsl(0,0%,0%)">In open consultative forums,  residents raised concerns ranging from youth unemployment and the cost of living to historical land injustices and marginalisation.</span></p><p><span style="color: hsl(0,0%,0%)">The tour heard directly from farming and pastoralist communities about neglect and exclusion, which are shaping the Ukatiba and Ukombozi agendas.</span></p><p><span style="color: hsl(0,0%,0%)">By the time the Caravan wound down in April, it had become the backbone of the campaign&#39;s ground game, with record single-day voter registration numbers in counties like Lamu. It lit the national that culminated in the State of the Nation address on June 16.</span></p><p><span style="color: hsl(0,0%,0%)">Listen below to what Kenyans had to say about the current state of life in Kenya today</span></p><figure class="media"><oembed url="https://youtu.be/XAZy5xHF9i0?si=6VloeK3Bshe99Cmw"></oembed></figure>	published	2026-06-16 20:49:00	1	f	2026-08-17 20:51:19	2026-08-17 20:53:59	{}	\N	\N	51	84	\N	\N
45	Gen Z Key to Kenya's Fate in 2027	gen-z-key-to-kenya-s-fate-in-2027	Blog	\N	/uploads/4fea5f3f-2418-4595-98fe-ce40d16a12f7.jpg	<p>No vote, no change. It really is that simple.&nbsp;</p><p>Kenya’s 2027 election stands to be shaped by one group more than any other: Gen Z. Young people are no longer just the future of this country. They are the present.</p><p>This election matters for a special reason. It will be the first big test of Gen Z as a voting bloc. Since the June 2024 protests, Gen Z have shown they can rise above tribal politics, a vice that has divided this country for decades. This could be the first election where that tribeless spirit shows up at the ballot box. But none of that matters if Gen Z are not registered to vote.</p><p><strong>Why Gen Z can sway 2027 : The Numbers&nbsp;</strong></p><p>The Independent Electoral and Boundaries Commission (IEBC) set out to register about 5.1 million first time Gen Z voters, people born between 2006 and 2009. During the Enhanced Continuous Voter Registration exercise, held from March 30 to April 28, 2026, the IEBC registered 2,345,476 new voters. Another 267,249 were added through the regular registration process. Most of these new voters were young people, a mix of Gen Z and millennials.</p><p><strong>Even with these numbers, the IEBC fell short of its target. That means the job is not finished. Between now and August 10, 2027, Gen Z's main task should be getting more of their peers registered as voters.</strong></p><p>Why do numbers matter? Because elections can be won by small margins. In 2022, William Ruto beat Raila Odinga by just 233,000 votes. &nbsp;Come 2027, the vote does not need to be that close. Gen Z alone has the numbers to decide it, close race or not. A margin of 233,000 votes is small compared to a bloc of 5 million young voters.&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Voter Registration Should not be a Burden</strong></p><p>Gen Z has every right to demand that the IEBC make voter registration easier to access. During the Ukatiba Caravan, UGM Presidential Aspirant David Maraga heard directly from young Kenyans across the country that the current registration centers were hard to reach. Many had to travel long distances to their constituency office just to register. In places like Kwale and the North Eastern region, registration centers were often hundreds of kilometers away. For many young people, the cost of getting there was simply too high.</p><p>Maraga put it plainly, “Access to voter registration is not a privilege. It is a right.” Improving access points is the only way to bring more young people into the electoral process.</p><p>He noted that pressure from the youth-led #NikoKadi movement is proof that this kind of demand works. The youth-led campaign pushed the IEBC to roll out the Enhanced Continuous Voter Registration exercise, which added about 2.3 million voters to the register.&nbsp;</p><p>Maraga praised this effort and called on young Kenyans to continue registering and on the IEBC to expand its registration schedule to meet the growing demand.</p><p><strong>Voting Is Just the Start</strong></p><p>Gen Z's role does not end at registration or even at casting a ballot. The Ol Kalou by-elections should be the model to follow. Young people should get behind strong grassroots campaigns for leaders who stand for integrity and good governance, across every seat up for election.</p><p>Registering is not enough. Gen Z must also show up on election day, and help drive high turnout among their peers as a top priority.</p><p>Beyond voting, Gen Z must act as watchdogs. That means guarding the vote, pushing for a fair and transparent process, and demanding a peaceful election both before and after the results are announced. Young people should also take on roles as election monitors and poll officials with the IEBC. This is not only about the presidency. It matters just as much for every elective seat, all the way down to the county assemblies.</p><p><strong>This Is Kenya's Defining Moment</strong></p><p>Sixty-three years after independence, 2027 offers Kenya a real chance to change direction. Away from poor governance, weak leadership, corruption, the looting of public resources, and the mismanagement that has kept Kenya and its people poor for far too long.</p><p>This is a call to Gen Z to take voter registration seriously, not just for the future of Kenya, but for the future they want for themselves. With less than a year to go before the 2027 election, this work must start now, and it is a responsibility every young Kenyan can share.</p><p><strong>Kenya cannot not change itself. Gen Z have the power to vote it into being.</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>By S. Maingi</p>	published	2026-08-14 13:58:00	1	t	2026-08-14 13:58:48	2026-08-19 17:23:09	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
73	Ukatiba	ukatiba	Newsletter	\N	/uploads/695d3369-0796-4bf3-a637-3b8f27b373ba.jpg	\N	published	2026-08-26 11:35:00	1	f	2026-08-29 11:36:18	2026-09-04 19:07:37	{"{\\"id\\": \\"2cdce77d-df43-4ee3-a753-4f7e79e4d267\\", \\"body\\": null, \\"heading\\": null, \\"position\\": 0, \\"image_urls\\": [\\"/uploads/b30b2021-f5e8-4f0d-b731-d23acd093294.pdf\\"]}"}	\N	Volume 04	50	50	\N	\N
44	Opposition Unity: Maraga Welcomes Talks on a Joint Flagbearer	opposition-unity-maraga-welcomes-talks-on-a-joint-flagbearer	Blog	\N	/uploads/92eeaf79-41b7-4d65-8c63-e5b7ef376808.jpg	<p><span style="color: black">On 16 June at Ufungamano House, David Maraga used his </span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KFRo5NZyJmU&amp;t=319s"><span style="color: black"><strong>State of the Nation </strong></span></a><span style="color: black">address to make one of his clearest statements yet on opposition unity. “I believe in opposition unity,” he told the gathering, but was careful to define the kind he means. </span></p><p><span style="color: black">“This opposition unity must be built on a convergence of values and not appetites,” he said, arguing that simply removing President Ruto is not enough if the next government repeats the same patterns of impunity under a different face.</span></p><p><span style="color: black">For Maraga, unity is a means to an end, not the end itself.  He has framed 2027 as a chance for opposition figures to come together around shared commitment to the rule of law, ending state capture, and permanently closing the door on any future leader weaponising public office for personal gain. </span></p><p><span style="color: black">Talks toward a joint flagbearer remain ongoing, and the campaign continues to position itself as a convening voice among opposition leaders, moral authorities and civil society, rather than a lone contender chasing a crowded field.</span></p><p> </p><p><span style="color: black">Watch the  full State of the Nation Address  below</span></p><figure class="media"><oembed url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KFRo5NZyJmU&amp;t=319s"></oembed></figure>	published	2026-06-16 18:00:00	1	f	2026-08-12 18:58:16	2026-08-14 14:50:45	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
46	State of the Nation: The Way Forward Speech by David Maraga	state-of-the-nation-the-way-forward-speech-by-david-maraga	Latest News	\N	/uploads/632d8b76-2776-4c3a-9d7d-5f9bcbd5a9bd.jpg	\N	published	2026-06-17 07:03:00	1	f	2026-08-14 15:04:58	2026-08-25 10:57:15	{"{\\"id\\": \\"bed29f58-6b60-4065-a4c4-75f044e2a0e9\\", \\"body\\": null, \\"heading\\": null, \\"position\\": 0, \\"image_urls\\": [\\"/uploads/3bed88c3-6a74-48d9-924b-2557e3f52813.jpg\\", \\"/uploads/e999c20a-a4fb-4762-ac07-fadebe5b7605.jpg\\", \\"/uploads/0faeab1f-9879-48be-91be-b644454c865c.jpg\\", \\"/uploads/b3eef52a-fd85-4586-a1dd-a95d235846f5.jpg\\"]}","{\\"id\\": \\"d0afbee4-e6b4-4295-8eb5-e11ff80529a5\\", \\"body\\": \\"<p><span style=\\\\\\"font-size: 12px\\\\\\"><i><strong>Top Left :</strong>  Hon Caleb Amisi and Hon Caroli Omondi <strong>Top Right :</strong> Hon . Martha Karua with Hon.  David Maraga <strong>Bottom Left:</strong>  Hon . David Maraga with UGM aspirants and party officials <strong> Bottom Right:</strong>  Mr. Reuben Kigame</i></span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: black\\\\\\">On 16 June, Maraga delivered a State of the Nation address at Ufungamano House in Nairobi, convening national moral authorities, opposition leaders, civil society, youth leaders, and professionals for a dialogue titled “State of the Nation: The Way Forward.” </span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: hsl(0,0%,0%)\\\\\\">The address was built on what the Ukatiba Caravan heard directly from Kenyans across all 43 counties. Billed as a non-partisan platform for charting the country&#39;s direction ahead of 2027, it named five painful realities plaguing Kenya: disregard for the rule of law, state capture and corruption, a ravaged economy, youth unemployment, and the collapse of health and education.</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: black\\\\\\">In his remarks, Maraga laid out the three-word doctrine that now defines his candidacy: Reset, Restore, Rebuild. Reset means a return to constitutionalism, where no one, not the president and not the pauper, sits above the law. Restore means reclaiming the humanity and dignity stripped from ordinary Kenyans by an extractive system. Rebuild means an economy that works for farmers, small businesses and the 1.2 million young people who graduate each year into a job market that cannot absorb them. He used the occasion to present his vision under the banner “<strong>Ukombozi 2027</strong>.” </span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: black\\\\\\">This is not abstract.</span><span style=\\\\\\"color: hsl(0,0%,0%)\\\\\\"> It is the same thread that runs through the campaign&#39;s core pillars. </span><span style=\\\\\\"color: black\\\\\\">Put simply, a Maraga presidency is a bet that a country can be run honestly, that stolen resources can  fund hospitals and schools instead of mansions, and that leadership can be judged by what it delivers rather than what it promises. As Maraga put it in closing, “It is time to stop dreaming about the Kenya we deserve and start building it.” That is the offer on the table for 2027</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: black\\\\\\">The Ufungamano House address is now regarded internally as the moment the Caravan&#39;s grassroots conversations were formally translated into a national agenda, and it remains one of the most referenced speeches of the campaign to date.</span></p><p><span style=\\\\\\"color: black\\\\\\">Watch the full speech here</span></p><figure class=\\\\\\"media\\\\\\"><oembed url=\\\\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KFRo5NZyJmU\\\\\\"></oembed></figure>\\", \\"heading\\": null, \\"position\\": 1, \\"image_urls\\": []}"}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
68	Opposition Unity: Maraga Welcomes Talks on Joint Flagbearer	opposition-unity-maraga-welcomes-talsk-on-joint-flagbearer	Latest News	\N	/uploads/bd827f98-18fc-410a-ab0a-8605647db5d7.jpg	<p><span style="color: black">With the August 2027 elections approaching, several new opposition factions have emerged, all working to unseat President Ruto. Many believe unity is the only real way forward, since history shows incumbents rarely lose especially with state resources and machinery on their side. Kenyans have echoed this, pushing opposition leaders to come together as they did in 2002, with a grand coalition that ultimately swept the incumbent party KANU, out of power.</span></p><p><span style="color: black">At  his State of the Nation address  at Ufungamano House on 16 June, Maraga made one of his clearest statements yet on opposition unity. “I believe in opposition unity,” he told the gathering, but was careful to define the kind he means. “<strong>This opposition unity must be built on a convergence of values and not appetites,</strong>” he said. He empasized that simply removing President Ruto was not enough if the next government repeats the same patterns of impunity under a different face.</span></p><p><span style="color: black">For Maraga, unity is a means to an end, not the end itself. He has framed 2027 as a chance for opposition figures to come together around shared commitment to the rule of law, ending state capture, and permanently closing the door on any future leader weaponising public office for personal gain. </span></p><p><span style="color: black">Talks toward a joint flagbearer remain ongoing, and the campaign continues to position itself as a convening voice among opposition leaders, moral authorities and civil society, rather than a lone contender chasing a crowded field.</span></p>	published	2026-06-18 13:32:00	1	f	2026-08-17 22:32:26	2026-08-19 12:56:09	{}	\N	\N	50	50	\N	\N
\.
