201	4333	Thuita	Kimani	Thuita Kimani	pkthuita@outlook.com	0768599366	Murang’a	Maragwa	Kimorori/wempa	Methi Nursery School	MARAGA 2027 — CREATIVE STRATEGY NOTE\nAn unsolicited idea worth considering\nBy Thuita Kimani\nCreative Director, MBT Creative Group\n\nThere’s a fundamental misalignment in how this campaign is currently being approached.\n\nYou are attempting to sell integrity using the same channels and tactics that have historically rewarded spectacle, populism, and noise. That gap is not just inefficient—it’s dangerous.\n\nAt a glance, the current approach feels less like a deliberate strategy and more like an under-leveraged opportunity. Whether that’s a function of limited creative direction or a lack of campaign architecture is unclear—but the outcome is the same: a strong candidate being positioned weakly.\n\nAnd that is a fixable problem.\n\nThe Core Observation\n\nThis is not a candidate who should be “marketed.”\nThis is a candidate who should be experienced, encountered, and trusted in layered environments.\n\nThe current digital-first, announcement-heavy approach (e.g., Paybill-driven engagement) reduces a statesman into a transaction.\n\nThat’s a brand misstep.\n\nA More Effective Direction: Grounded Influence Ecosystems\n\nInstead of relying primarily on broadcast messaging, the campaign can unlock significantly more traction through embedded community presence and cultural proximity.\n\nThis means shifting from:\n\n“See the candidate” → to → “Be in spaces where the candidate matters.”\n\nPractical execution would look like:\n\n1. Faith & Values Channels (High-Trust Networks)\nStructured presence in church ecosystems—not as political rallies, but as value-aligned engagements\nYouth fellowships and Gen Z church communities (already organized, already influential)\nNarrative positioning: Ukatiba as a moral framework, not just a legal one\n\nThis is not exploitation—it’s alignment. These are some of the most disciplined and mobilizable communities in the country.\n\n2. Fundraisers as Narrative Stages (Not Just Capital Events)\nTransform fundraisers into storytelling environments\nCurate who is in the room: professionals, youth leaders, diaspora connectors\nEach event becomes a content engine (short-form, testimonials, influence loops)\n\nRight now, fundraisers are being treated as financial tools.\nThey should be treated as perception architecture.\n\n3. Golf & Professional Circles (Power Without Noise)\nGolf tournaments, private networking events, industry roundtables\nQuiet rooms where decision-makers validate credibility\n\nThis aligns perfectly with a “sober leadership” brand—presence without performance.\n\n4. Localized Community Activations\nKanisa drives, community initiatives, education-linked engagements\nNot branded as “campaign stops” but as participatory civic moments\n\nThe goal is simple:\nReduce political distance. Increase human proximity.\n\nThe Strategic Gap\n\nThere is currently an over-reliance on:\n\nDirect messaging\nDigital announcements\nSurface-level engagement\n\nAnd an underinvestment in:\n\nCultural embedding\nPhysical presence in trusted spaces\nNarrative depth through lived interaction\n\nThat imbalance creates a perception problem.\n\nThe Underlying Question\n\nWhen a candidate of this caliber is underperforming in visibility and traction, two possibilities emerge:\n\nIt is a resourcing issue\nOr it is a creative and strategic execution gap\n\nEither way, the campaign is not yet operating at the level required to convert credibility into votes.\n\nFinal Thought\n\nA candidate built on discipline, law, and principle cannot be run on improvisation.\n\nThe opportunity here is not to make the campaign louder—\nIt is to make it more intentional, more embedded, and more strategically present.\n\nThere is a winning path.\nIt just requires a shift from campaigning at people → to campaigning within their realities.\n\nNo ask attached to this. Just a perspective from a creative strategist observing a high-potential campaign that can be significantly stronger with the right structural adjustments.	2026-04-01	2026-04-01	2026-06-14 18:36:59	2026-06-14 18:36:59
