387	4140	Oloibon	Mbatian	Oloibon Mbatian	oloibon.mbatian@opentally.org	+254700000000	Nairobi City	Dagoretti South	Mutu-ini	Kirigu Primary School	Dear Chief Justice Maraga,\n\nI hope you are well.\n\nI’m writing to you with deep respect for the role you have played in Kenya’s constitutional journey, and with a matter that sits directly within the principles you have defended throughout your public life.\n\nFew moments in our country’s history have demonstrated the power of the Constitution as clearly as the 2017 Supreme Court decision. It was a reminder that the law is not symbolic. It is meant to be lived, tested, and upheld, even under pressure.\n\nAnd yet, since then, one question has quietly remained unresolved.\n\nHow do ordinary citizens, outside of courtrooms and institutions, actually verify that an election meets the constitutional standard?\n\nNot in theory. Not through legal argument. But in practice.\n\nKenya’s Constitution is clear. Elections must be simple, accurate, verifiable, secure, accountable, and transparent. The word “verifiable” carries weight. It implies that any interested party should be able to confirm the result for themselves, using evidence they can see and understand.\n\nToday, that is still not something most Kenyans can realistically do.\n\nI have been working on a project called OpenTally.\n\nYou can view the vision here: https://opentally.org\n\nWhat is available there is not yet the system itself, but a clear outline of what we intend to build before the next election cycle.\n\nAt its core, OpenTally is a public verification infrastructure. It allows election results to be traced directly back to the original polling station forms, in a way that is open, structured, and accessible to anyone. It does not replace the electoral commission and it does not declare results. It simply makes it possible for the public to verify what has been declared, using the same source documents the law recognizes as final.\n\nIn many ways, it is a practical extension of the principles established in decisions such as Maina Kiai. The forms are the result. This system simply makes it possible for that truth to be seen, aggregated, and checked at scale.\n\n---\n\nI am reaching out to you because of both your past role and your present position.\n\nAs a former Chief Justice, you have helped define the legal standards that govern our elections. As someone now considering national leadership, you also understand the political realities that surround those standards.\n\nOpenTally sits at the intersection of those two worlds.\n\nIt reduces the gap between law and practice. It makes verification something that does not depend on litigation after the fact, but something that is available in real time to citizens, parties, observers, and institutions.\n\nIt also addresses a very practical challenge. Political actors invest heavily in parallel tallying systems, agents, and internal verification processes, and still find themselves in contested outcomes. A shared, transparent verification layer reduces that burden and replaces it with something everyone can see and rely on.\n\n---\n\nI also want to be clear.\n\nThis project will move forward.\n\nThere is growing recognition, both within Kenya and beyond, that the country needs a better way to verify elections before the next cycle. Conversations are already underway, and support is beginning to align around this need.\n\nWhat I am offering you is the opportunity to be part of it early. To stand behind a system that gives real meaning to the constitutional requirement of verifiability, and to be associated with building something that strengthens trust in our democracy at a foundational level.\n\nGiven your history, your voice on a matter like this carries particular weight.\n\n---\n\nThis is not a political request. It is a civic one.\n\nIf you are open to it, I would value the opportunity to speak with you or someone on your team and walk through the idea in more detail.\n\nThank you for your time, and for your continued contribution to Kenya’s constitutional and public life.\n\nWarm regards,\nOloibon Mbatian	2026-03-26	2026-03-26	2026-06-14 18:37:03	2026-06-14 18:37:03
