766	3807	June	Kibue	June Kibue	jkibue3@gmail.com	0721423701	Nairobi City	Westlands	Karura	City Park Market	Strategy Report: Mobilizing Youth Candidates for November 2025 By-Elections (Groundwork for 2027)\nKenya’s upcoming November 27, 2025, by-elections (23–24 seats, including one Senate and six National Assembly seats) present a critical “litmus test” of political trends ahead of the 2027 general election. Kenya’s demographics overwhelmingly favor young voters: about 80% of Kenyans are age 35 or younger, and the 18–35 cohort alone comprises over one-third of the population. Yet this youth majority is deeply dissatisfied – recent surveys show young Kenyans regard economic hardship, lack of jobs, and corruption as top problems, and a majority say the country is moving in the wrong direction. Gen-Z (roughly 18–25) activists have already protested en masse against high living costs, corruption and police brutality, even chanting “Ruto one-term” as they confronted government buildings. In short, the youth are energised, discontented, and (crucially) technically savvy – organizing informally via social media outside traditional party channels.\nOpportunity: If Team Maraga can channel this youth energy into electoral participation, the by-elections will both strengthen his brand and seed a supportive bench in Parliament/Senate for 2027. Fielding young candidates in every race will (a) demonstrate that Maraga’s movement truly embraces the youth majority, (b) give young activists a stake in his success, and (c) begin building a cohort of aligned legislators. We must capitalize on the moment. The plan below lays out the strategic pillars and concrete steps to recruit, support and elect youth candidates across all by-election seats as a rehearsal for the general election.\n1. Context & Rationale\n•\tYouth Majority and Dissatisfaction: Demographic data confirm Kenya’s “youth bulge” – roughly 75–80% of citizens are under 35. This generation is more educated than their elders but also more likely unemployed. The Afrobarometer survey finds youth list health, cost of living, jobs and corruption as their top concerns. In 2024–2025 thousands of Gen-Zers took to the streets demanding reform, signaling profound restlessness. Crucially, many have no loyalty to old parties and coordinate via social media networks.\n•\tBy-Elections as Litmus Test: The IEBC has scheduled all pending by-elections for Nov 27, 2025. These 24 contests (1 Senate, 6 MPs, 17 ward seats) are widely seen as a rehearsal for 2027. Success here can produce tangible legislative gains now and valuable experience. In particular, winning these seats – or even running strong youth campaigns – will signal youth-powered momentum and begin to fill Parliament and county assemblies with committed reformers.\n•\tMaraga’s Appeal to Youth: Former Chief Justice David Maraga has cultivated a “clean,” anti-corruption image and publicly condemned police brutality against protesters. Capital FM reports that some Gen-Z activists already admire his stance. We must build on this credibility: by aligning with genuine youth voices (not just token support), Maraga can bridge the gap between street protest and formal politics.\n2. Strategic Pillars\nTo harness youth energy in all by-election races, Team Maraga’s campaign should pursue multiple integrated strategies:\n•\tCandidate Recruitment & Support:\n•\tIdentify and Recruit Youth Leaders: Immediately map each of the 23 target constituencies and wards to find promising young community leaders, activists or recent graduates (ages 22–35). Focus on individuals with local credibility, language skills, or issue expertise (e.g. unemployment, education). Use local networks, student associations, and social-media outreach to compile a talent pool.\n•\tNomination Compliance: Ensure all youth candidates meet IEBC requirements. For party-aligned runs, submit lists of authorized agents and primary winners by Sept 17, 2025, in line with IEBC guidelines. For independents, verify they were unaffiliated for 3+ months and file by Sept 17 as well. Communicate deadlines (Sept 17 nomination filings, Oct 8–9 nominations, campaigns Oct 8–Nov 24) so no candidacy slips through the cracks.\n•\tTraining and Vetting: Provide intensive pre-campaign training to each youth candidate. Workshops should cover: campaign management (organizing volunteers, door-to-door canvassing), legal compliance (electoral laws), communication skills (public speaking, debates, social media use), and policy basics (Kenyan constitution, local governance issues). Pair each candidate with an experienced mentor (possibly a sympathetic former MP or activist) for ongoing guidance. Emphasize discipline and integrity to reinforce Maraga’s clean-image brand.\n•\tResource Support: Pool campaign resources for youth candidates. Facilitate low-interest loans or crowdfunding for expenses (printing flyers, transport). Connect them with trusted local fundraisers or diaspora Kenyans willing to support youth empowerment (consistent with Maraga’s pledge to avoid wealthy backers). Create a “campaign war room” to allocate volunteers (e.g. university students, community organizers) to priority constituencies. This will help youth candidates punch above their weight.\n•\tYouth-Centric Messaging & Platform:\n•\tIssue-Driven Narrative: Craft a coherent message around core youth concerns. Emphasize how electing young leaders will tackle their priorities – for example: guarantee affordable healthcare, fight the cost-of-living crisis, create youth-focused job programs, and stamp out corruption that diverts resources. Use data: Afrobarometer finds young Kenyans list health, living costs, jobs and corruption as top government action areas. Align Maraga’s campaign platform to these priorities (e.g. propose a “Youth Jobs Initiative” or accountability measures). Weave Maraga’s integrity and legal expertise into this narrative (“clean leadership” that enforces rights for ordinary people).\n•\tPositive National Vision: Frame the youth slate as part of a larger renewal. Stress unity and a forward-looking agenda beyond tribal politics (Maraga rejects “tribal math”). Connect local races to national change: “Voting in young champions now is the first step toward a better Kenya in 2027.” Counter cynicism by highlighting optimism – Afrobarometer notes many youth do expect improvements. Convey that their vote can start the improvement they seek.\n•\tLanguage and Style: Use youth-friendly language and platforms. Messages should be concise and bold – e.g. “Kenya’s Future: In Young Hands” or “Nyayo ya Vijana” (youth path). Employ visuals and memes resonating with Gen-Z (without alienating older youth). Avoid dense policy speak; instead use stories of fellow young Kenyans (perhaps existing civic leaders or entrepreneurs) to illustrate points.\n•\tCandidate Personal Branding: Help each youth candidate craft a personal profile highlighting their local roots and aspirations. Encourage them to share relatable stories (community involvement, small business startup, university projects). This builds emotional connection with voters. Also ensure they all publicly align with Maraga’s core message (e.g. in campaign materials mention “A vote for [Name] is a vote for David Maraga’s vision of justice and opportunity”).\n•\tGrassroots Mobilization & Digital Outreach:\n•\tLocal Organizing: Set up campaign desks or contacts in each of the 23 areas. Recruit local volunteers (youth and sympathetic elders) as constituency committees. They will organize grassroots events: youth rallies, community service projects (clean-ups, clinics), and issue forums. Use these gatherings to register supporters and answer questions. Since by-election campaigns are short (Oct 8 – Nov 24), start low-key organizing now (informational meetups, door-knocking in neighborhoods with high youth populations) to build momentum before official campaign period.\n•\tDigital Campaign: Exploit social media aggressively. According to Reuters, Kenyan Gen Z “mobilise amorphously through social media, bypassing opposition parties”. Key actions: launch a coordinated online campaign (#YouthWave2025 or similar), create viral content (videos, TikToks) featuring youth candidates addressing peers, and schedule live Q&A sessions on Facebook/YouTube. Provide each candidate with access to a central social media manager or digital toolkit (sample graphics, approved talking points). Use WhatsApp broadcast lists and SMS groups to share campaign updates (Kenya has high mobile penetration even among rural youth).\n•\tYouth Influencers & Media: Partner with popular youth figures (musicians, sports personalities, social media influencers) who are sympathetic to reform. Invite them to appear with youth candidates or share endorsements. Pitch stories to local radio (many Kenyans listen to vernacular FM radio) and online outlets about “Kenya’s Youth Rising”. Traditional media can be used to amplify the youth voice – seek interviews for candidates on TV, or publish op-eds by young activists in dailies.\n•\tVoter Education: Since youth often feel disconnected from the voting process, run voter education drives: simple guides on how and where to vote, deadlines for registration, etc. Emphasize that by-elections offer a realistic chance for youth-led change. This combats apathy: if young people believe their candidates can win, they are more likely to turn out.\n•\tAlliances & Legitimacy Building:\n•\tEngage Youth Organizations: Work with formal youth bodies (e.g. National Youth Council county chapters) and civil society groups that focus on youth empowerment. Invite them to observe primaries or endorse a youth manifesto. Their involvement will boost credibility. For example, incorporate items from the Kenya Youth Manifesto (2022–2027) on education, jobs and governance into the campaign platform (they’re widely shared among youth activists).\n•\tCross-Community Outreach: Bridge tribal/ethnic divides by underscoring shared youth interests. Encourage youth candidates to organize joint events with peers from other communities when possible. Highlight stories of youth cooperation (e.g. mixed-ethnic volunteer projects). Since Maraga himself appeals beyond tribal bases, this reinforces a national youth coalition.\n•\tTransitioning Protest to Policy: Channel recent protest energy into positive participation. Reach out to known protest organizers, inviting them to “bring your ideas into the halls of power” by supporting a youth candidate. Offer a platform for their grievances to be addressed through legislation rather than the streets. This can neutralize any distrust: many activists “won’t be fooled by clean suits and big words”, so show tangible inclusion (e.g. have one candidate commit to holding a townhall on policing issues).\n•\tHighlight Democracy & Oversight: Emphasize that these by-elections are about filling long-vacant seats and making government more representative. Work with election observers (local NGOs, faith groups) to monitor polling. A clean election result will legitimize youth victories. If any intimidation occurs (e.g. government labeling youth as “anarchists” as happened before), swiftly use media to expose it and rally support for youth candidates’ democratic rights.\n•\tMonitoring & Adaptive Management:\n•\tFeedback Loops: Set up regular coordination calls or online forums so all youth campaigns can share progress. Track metrics: voter registration among youth, rally attendance, online engagement, and any early polling data. If a particular message or event format is gaining traction, replicate it elsewhere. Conversely, if pushback occurs (e.g. a misinformation campaign), respond quickly with fact-checks.\n•\tPost-Election Handoff: If youth candidates win seats, prepare them for legislative roles (training on lawmaking). If they lose, frame strong vote-share gains as success (e.g. “44% of voters in X ward supported Youth Empowerment” can be spun as influence). Document lessons learned in each constituency to refine the 2027 strategy. Building on the by-elections, designate all youth candidates as part of a “Maraga Youth Caucus” that will carry forward the movement’s agenda into 2027 – even outside formal office.\n3. Key Takeaways\n•\tYouth are Kingmakers: By targeting the 18–35 age group (roughly 36% of Kenya’s population and ~80% under 35), Team Maraga taps into the country’s largest bloc. Ignoring youth means ceding ground to rivals; engaging them can decide both local races and the national mood.\n•\tBy-elections as a Springboard: Winning youth support now not only helps Maraga’s momentum but also begins rebuilding Parliament/Senate in his favor. The 2025 mini-polls are officially a “litmus test” for 2027 – let them showcase a groundswell of young leadership.\n•\tFocus on Substance and Vision: Young voters want more than slogans. Address their concrete issues (jobs, costs, corruption) in the campaign and show how youthful legislators will push real solutions. Maraga’s anti-corruption legacy resonates with Gen Z; reinforce it through youth candidate platforms.\n•\tMulti-Channel Mobilization: Combine grassroots organizing with digital savvy. Leveraging social media is crucial since Gen Z “bypasses opposition parties” to self-organize online. Engage them where they are, from smartphones to shags of major universities and towns.\n•\tBuild for the Long Haul: This is just the beginning of 18 months of electioneering. Use the by-election campaigns to refine the youth outreach model (training, messaging, logistics). Every volunteer and voter touched now is an asset for 2027.\nIn sum, Team Maraga must actively encourage, recruit, and support a full slate of energetic young candidates in the November by-elections. This strategy – deeply grounded in Kenya’s demographic and political realities – will test the organizing model and galvanize youth to vote for change. By showcasing youth power today, we lay a solid foundation for winning the presidency and a reform-minded Parliament in 2027.\nSources: Recent reports and analyses of Kenyan youth politics and elections were used to inform this strategy.\n\nStrategic Report: Youth-Centered By-Election Strategy for Team Maraga 2025-2027\nExecutive Summary\nThe upcoming November 2025 by-elections present Team Maraga with a critical strategic opportunity to test and demonstrate the political power of Kenya's youth ahead of the 2027 presidential election. With 23 seats in contention—including 16 county assembly wards, 6 national assembly constituencies, and 1 senate seat—these by-elections serve as an ideal proving ground for youth-driven political mobilization. \nStrategic Recommendation: Team Maraga should prioritize fielding young candidates in all 23 by-election seats and mobilize comprehensive youth support campaigns. This approach will test youth electoral power, build organizational capacity, and establish the foundation for a competent Parliament and Senate essential for implementing transformative governance after the 2027 presidential victory.\nThe Strategic Context\nYouth Demographics and Electoral Power\nKenya's youth represent a formidable but underutilized political force. Comprising 75% of the national population and 35% of the active workforce, the youth demographic (18-35 years) holds immense electoral potential. However, this power remains largely untapped due to historical political exclusion and disillusionment. \nThe 2022 general election exposed concerning trends in youth political engagement:\n•\tYouth voter registration declined by 5.27% compared to 2017 \n•\tOnly 39.84% of registered voters were under 35\n•\tYouth represented less than 10% of actual votes cast despite being eligible to vote\n•\tOverall voter turnout hit a 15-year low at 65%\nThe Gen-Z Awakening: A Game-Changing Factor\nThe 2024 Gen-Z protests fundamentally altered Kenya's political landscape, demonstrating unprecedented youth political consciousness and organizational capacity. Key characteristics of this movement include: \nDigital-Native Organization: Protesters leveraged social media platforms like TikTok and X (Twitter) with hashtags such as #RejectFinanceBill2024 and #OccupyParliament, creating a decentralized yet coordinated nationwide mobilization. \nIssue-Based Activism: The movement transcended traditional ethnic and regional boundaries, focusing on governance accountability, corruption, cost of living, and economic justice.\nSustained Engagement: Despite government crackdowns that resulted in at least 60 deaths, the youth movement maintained momentum and political relevance.\nAnti-Establishment Sentiment: Young Kenyans demonstrated clear rejection of traditional political patronage and demand for authentic leadership.\nCurrent Economic and Political Climate\nKenya's youth face severe economic challenges that fuel their political engagement:\n•\tYouth unemployment stands at 67% for those aged 15-34\n•\tOver 1 million young people enter the job market annually without adequate skills\n•\t10 million youth in the active labor force face underemployment or unemployment\n•\tMedian wage for employed youth is only KSh 6,000\nThese economic pressures, combined with governance failures, have created a perfect storm for youth political mobilization that Team Maraga can harness.\nStrategic Framework: By-Elections as Youth Power Testing Ground\nRationale for Youth-Centered Strategy\nDemographic Advantage: With youth representing the majority of Kenya's population, mobilizing this demographic creates a sustainable electoral base that transcends traditional ethnic and regional politics. \nMomentum Utilization: The Gen-Z protest movement has already demonstrated organizational capacity and political consciousness. By-elections provide a channel to convert this energy into electoral success. \nInstitution Building: Successfully electing young candidates will begin building the competent Parliament and Senate necessary for implementing Maraga's transformative agenda post-2027. \nMedia and Public Attention: Youth candidates will generate significant media coverage and public interest, amplifying Team Maraga's message and vision. \n\nThe 23 By-Election Seats: Strategic Opportunities\nThe November 27, 2025 by-elections encompass: \nCounty Assembly Wards (16 seats):\n•\tAngata Nanyokie (Samburu)\n•\tChemundu/Kapng'etuny (Nandi)\n•\tChewani (Tana River)\n•\tFafi (Garissa)\n•\tKariobangi North (Nairobi)\n•\tKisa East (Kakamega)\n•\tMetkei (Elgeyo Marakwet)\n•\tMumbuni North (Machakos)\n•\tNarok Town (Narok)\n•\tPurko (Kajiado)\n•\tTembelio (Uasin Gishu)\n•\tNyansiongo, Nyamaiya, Ekerenyo (Nyamira - 3 wards)\n•\tLake Zone, Nanaam (Turkana - 2 wards)\nNational Assembly Seats (6 constituencies):\n•\tBanissa\n•\tKasipul\n•\tMagarini\n•\tMalava\n•\tMbeere North\n•\tUgunja\nSenate Seat (1):\n•\tBaringo County\n\nImplementation Strategy\nPhase 1: Candidate Identification and Development (August-September 2025)\nTarget Profile: Identify candidates aged 25-35 with:\n•\tStrong educational backgrounds\n•\tCommunity leadership experience\n•\tDigital literacy and social media presence\n•\tClean records free from corruption allegations\n•\tAlignment with Maraga's governance vision\nCapacity Building: Implement intensive training programs covering:\n•\tPublic policy analysis\n•\tLegislative processes\n•\tCampaign management\n•\tDigital communication strategies\n•\tGovernance and accountability principles\nMentorship Program: Pair young candidates with experienced political figures aligned with Team Maraga's vision for guidance and support.\nPhase 2: Grassroots Mobilization (September-November 2025)\nDigital-First Campaign Strategy: Leverage platforms where youth are most active:\n•\tTikTok for viral content creation\n•\tX (Twitter) for real-time engagement\n•\tWhatsApp for direct community organizing\n•\tFacebook for broader demographic reach \nCommunity Organizing: Establish youth volunteer networks in each constituency:\n•\tUniversity and college student groups\n•\tYoung professionals associations\n•\tYouth-focused community organizations\n•\tDigital activism networks from the Gen-Z movement\nIssue-Based Messaging: Center campaigns on issues that resonate with youth:\n•\tEmployment creation and economic opportunities\n•\tEducation reform and access\n•\tHealthcare improvement\n•\tAnti-corruption initiatives\n•\tDigital governance and transparency[17][18][16]\nPhase 3: Electoral Strategy (October-November 2025)\nVoter Registration Drives: Address the historical challenge of low youth voter registration through:\n•\tMobile registration units at universities and colleges\n•\tDigital awareness campaigns about the importance of voting\n•\tPartnerships with civil society organizations \nGet-Out-The-Vote (GOTV) Operations: Implement comprehensive GOTV strategies:\n•\tPeer-to-peer mobilization networks\n•\tTransportation assistance to polling stations\n•\tDigital reminders and voter education\n•\tElection day monitoring and support \nCoalition Building: Form strategic alliances with:\n•\tCivil society organizations focused on governance\n•\tReligious groups supportive of reform\n•\tBusiness associations seeking good governance\n•\tInternational development partners \n\n\nExpected Outcomes and Benefits\nImmediate Benefits (2025-2026)\nElectoral Performance Measurement: By-election results will provide concrete data on youth mobilization capacity and electoral preferences, informing 2027 strategy refinements.\nOrganizational Capacity Building: Campaign operations will develop crucial infrastructure for the 2027 presidential campaign, including volunteer networks, digital platforms, and fundraising mechanisms.\nMedia Positioning: Success in by-elections will establish Team Maraga as the authentic voice of Kenya's youth, differentiating from traditional political establishments.\nParliamentary Representation: Elected young candidates will begin advancing Team Maraga's legislative agenda immediately, creating early policy wins and demonstrating competence.\nLong-term Strategic Value (2027 and Beyond)\nPresidential Campaign Foundation: Successful youth mobilization in by-elections will create a robust organizational base for the 2027 presidential campaign across multiple constituencies.\nLegislative Partnership: Young MPs and MCAs will form the core of a competent parliamentary team essential for implementing the presidential policy agenda. \nInstitutional Reform: Youth representatives will champion governance reforms, transparency initiatives, and anti-corruption measures that align with Maraga's vision.\nGenerational Political Shift: Success will accelerate the broader transformation of Kenyan politics from ethnic-based to issue-based governance, benefiting long-term democratic development.\nRisk Mitigation Strategies\nManaging Resource Constraints\nCrowd-funding Campaigns: Leverage digital platforms to raise campaign funds from the youth demographic and diaspora communities.\nVolunteer-Based Operations: Rely heavily on young volunteers rather than paid staff to minimize operational costs while maximizing grassroots engagement.\nStrategic Partnerships: Collaborate with organizations and individuals who support governance reform to share resources and expertise.\nAddressing Political Opposition\nPreparedness for Negative Campaigns: Anticipate attempts to characterize youth candidates as inexperienced or radical. Counter with concrete policy proposals and demonstrated competence.\nLegal Compliance: Ensure all campaign activities strictly adhere to electoral laws to avoid disqualification or legal challenges.\nCoalition Diversification: Build support beyond youth demographics to demonstrate broad-based appeal and political maturity.\nOrganizational Challenges\nCoordination Systems: Implement robust communication and coordination systems across all 23 constituencies to ensure consistent messaging and strategy execution.\nLeadership Development: Provide ongoing support and training to young candidates throughout the campaign period and after election.\nSustainability Planning: Develop plans for maintaining momentum and organization between by-elections and the 2027 general election.\nConclusion\nThe November 2025 by-elections represent a pivotal moment for Team Maraga to demonstrate that Kenya's youth are ready to transform the country's political landscape. By fielding competent young candidates in all 23 seats and mobilizing comprehensive youth support, Team Maraga can achieve multiple strategic objectives: testing electoral capabilities, building organizational capacity, establishing legislative partnerships, and positioning for presidential victory in 2027.\nThe Gen-Z protest movement has already proven that Kenya's youth possess the political consciousness, organizational ability, and determination necessary for electoral success. The by-elections provide the perfect opportunity to channel this energy into concrete political gains that will benefit both the immediate governance agenda and long-term democratic transformation.\nSuccess in these by-elections will send a powerful message that change is not only possible but inevitable, establishing Team Maraga as the authentic voice of Kenya's future and creating the foundation for transformative governance after 2027. The youth have already demonstrated their power in the streets; now it is time to demonstrate their power at the ballot box.	2025-08-21	2025-08-21	2026-06-14 18:37:10	2026-06-14 18:37:10
